{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1791980000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase6a-r2.prompt.md collab/1791980000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase6a-r2.report.md collab/1791940000000-reviewer-srvm-phase6a.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-11 09:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-11 16:40:00 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Task: 6a round 2 -- kill the surviving mutants (continuing your thread)\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch `srvm-phase6a`, HEAD 4b0e735e. Do NOT `git add`/commit. Read the review in full: `collab/1791940000000-reviewer-srvm-phase6a.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md` (44 mutants, 29 killed, 15 survived; findings 1-5 and the \"Required before merge\" section are your definition of done). Its mutation script is `collab/6a-reviewer-mutants.py` with a table in `collab/6a-reviewer-mutants-main.json`; reuse the reviewer's mutants.\n\nSOURCE CHANGES ARE AUTHORIZED for the defects below (report each as a source change with evidence), and TEST changes for the rest.\n\nORCHESTRATOR RULINGS on the reviewer's open points:\n- Finding 1 (HIGH, Dart): fix `rows_test.cljc:238` by chaining single-key `assoc`s on the vector (cljd inlines multi-key assoc on a vector to a `-conj` of a map); search the test files for any other multi-key assoc on a vector or nil. The orchestrator re-runs Dart.\n- Findings 2 and 3 (host exceptions escape as non-refusals): fix in `rows.cljc` as the reviewer proposes: validate census `:C` slot keys against `c-[1-9][0-9]*` in `structural?` (refuse `:slot-kind`) and parse digits with a guard, not the EDN reader; in `gate` require `(and (vector? installs) (every? keyword? installs))`, refusing `:task-shape`. Add a refusal test for each probe.\n- Finding 6 (marker representation): RULING: keep plain-map markers and REFUSE a guest map whose shape collides with a marker (exactly the key `:yin.k/row` or `:yin.k/cell` as a sole key) as `:yin.k/non-portable` on encode and on decode; document the refusal in the namespace docstring. Test both directions.\n- Finding 7: S4 refuses a present `:yin.k/store-of nil` (`:env-shape`); test it.\n- Finding 8: RULING: the census is ordered numerically by n (design section 3's reading); record this reading in the namespace docstring and add a >=10-cell case (c-10 sorts after c-9).\n- Findings 9 and 11: notes only (state them in the report as known limitations). Finding 10: pass the caller's `budget` to the install-child numbering if it is a one-token change; otherwise leave and say so. Finding 13(b)-(d): correct the wording of the acceptance-item comment/test names that overclaim.\n Take a verified byte backup of each source file into `collab/` BEFORE any mutant; restore from it (`cmp` and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/`).\n\nFor EVERY surviving mutant (R06, R07, R10, R12, R17, R21, R24, R25, R26, R29, R30, R33, R35, R45, R46) and for findings 1-5: add or strengthen a named test, re-apply the mutant, confirm it is KILLED by a named test, restore. Findings 4 and 5 specifically: (4) acceptance item 10's unordered-container permutation is vacuous: emit one task whose `:set`/`:map` slots are listed in reversed abstract order under the IDENTITY correspondence and assert equal roots (streams, not only cells, in the set); (5) pin at least one root id (the reviewer's probes: `(fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)` -> `:segment/blake3-be82d50b14804bb7a69103631b9c45759b49b67dbb3cebe75a1259fed6b4a937`; `(frame->rows ft/worked-frame)` -> `:segment/blake3-c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d`; recompute them yourself and report if they differ) and add a fixture with two refinement-equivalent but non-automorphic cells (the draft section 6 triangle/square case) whose chosen leaf is asserted. Anything you judge an EQUIVALENT mutant: argue it explicitly with evidence, do not just omit it.\n\nVerify, foreground, literal `-n` flags: JVM for frame-test, rows-test and the semantic-register namespaces as in your first report; Node as in your first report; kondo and cljstyle. Dart: orchestrator runs it.\n\nReport to `collab/1791980000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase6a-r2.report.md`, beginning exactly:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS Asia/Bangkok>\nA table: mutant | killed by which named test (or equivalent, with argument) ; per finding what changed; exact lane counts. Do not claim runs that did not occur.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 09:31:56 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 16:31:56 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Slice 6a round 2: surviving mutants and findings 1-13\n\nEngineer: claude-opus-5-5 (fresh session; the round-1 author, codex gpt-6.1-sol, is out of credits). Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch `srvm-phase6a`, HEAD 4b0e735e. Nothing staged or committed. The clock tool's time (09:31:56 GMT) is earlier than the brief's Created-GMT marker (09:40); I used the clock and did not invent a later time.\n\nState found on arrival: `src/` and `test/` were clean against HEAD. An earlier round-2 attempt (`...-r2.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log`) had been terminated mid-thought with no edits. `...-r2.opus.stdout.log` is empty.\n\n## Outcome\n\n- All 15 surviving reviewer mutants are now **KILLED**, each by a named test, using the reviewer's own needles and replacements unchanged.\n- Each round-2 source fix also has a revert-mutant (F2a, F2b, F3, F6a, F6b, F7), and all six are KILLED.\n- None of the 15 is an equivalent mutant. I argued none away.\n- Lanes on JVM and Node are green, and kondo and cljstyle are clean (counts below).\n- **Dart was not run by me**; per the brief the orchestrator runs it. Finding 1's fix can only be confirmed on Dart.\n- **New finding (MEDIUM, not fixed): out of memory, not a resource refusal.** The draft §6 triangle/square fixture (7 equal-body cells) runs the JVM out of heap (`java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space` in `canonical-numbering`/`refine`/`classes`) under the default budget. The §6 labels nest the other cells' labels each round, so their bytes grow geometrically. The `:work`/`:search` budgets count calls, not label bytes, so the budget never fires. I observed this in a run of `canonical-roots-are-pinned`, before I replaced that fixture.\n  - For the finding-5 fixture I therefore used the smallest instance of the same phenomenon: a directed loop plus a directed 2-cycle, three cells.\n  - A fix (for example, charging label bytes to `:work`) changes local bounds only, not canonical bytes. It was not authorized, so it is left for the Architect.\n\n## Backups and restoration\n\n- **Before any edit:** I backed up both sources byte-for-byte to `collab/6a-r2-backups/pre/{frame,rows}.cljc`. Both `cmp` were 0 and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` was 0 at that point (output `PRE_BACKUP_OK`).\n- **Before any mutant:** I backed up the edited sources to `collab/6a-r2-backups/{frame,rows}.cljc` (`cmp` 0). The authorized source diff is saved as `collab/6a-r2-backups/src.diff` (128 lines).\n- **Restoration checks.** Because the round-2 source changes are authorized and uncommitted, `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` is 1 by construction. The harness records it on every run anyway. As the real restoration check it requires that `cmp` against the r2 backup is 0, and that `git diff HEAD -- src/` is byte-equal to the saved diff. Both held after every one of the 22 mutant runs (`restore_cmp: 0`, `restore_src_diff_equals_saved: true`).\n- **Final state:** both sources `cmp`-equal the r2 backups.\n- **Harness:** `collab/6a-r2-mutants.py`. It reads the reviewer's `MUTANTS` table from `collab/6a-reviewer-mutants.py`, so the needles are identical.\n- **Commands:** each run is `clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>` with literal names, foreground.\n- **Logs:** per-mutant logs are `collab/6a-r2-<ID>.log`. The tables are `collab/6a-r2-mutants-R06-R07-R10-R12-R17-R21-R24-R25-R26-R29.json`, `...-F2a-F2b-F3-F6a-F6b-F7-R30-R33-R35-R45-R46.json` and `...-R35.json`.\n\n## Mutant table\n\nThe reviewer's 15 survivors (needles unchanged):\n\n| Mutant | Killed by | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| R06 S2 non-vector K accepted | `frame-test/rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper` (\"S2 map K\", \"S2 nil K\") | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| R07 C2 `pc > 0` dropped | `frame-test/rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper` (\"C2 pc 0\") | KILLED, 1 error (the mutant validator throws on `(dec 0)` in C3) |\n| R10 A5/A6 order swapped | `frame-test/rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper` (\"A5 before A6\", window `{3 :x}`) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R12 P2 `:park`/`:current-continuation` admit `:next` | `frame-test/rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper` (\"P2 park\", \"P2 current-continuation\") | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| R17 search keeps first leaf | `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: renamed loop fixture gives root `…315b1a17…` instead of `…8899f95d…`, and the loop cell gets c-1 instead of c-3 | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| R21 numbering reversed | `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned` (pinned root) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R24 extra top-level wire key | `rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes` (`(assoc wire :extra 1)` → `:row-shape`) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R25 X1 after waits | `rows-test/task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`: a task with both reserved `yin/def` in free-env and a defective wait refuses `:reserved-name` | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R26 `:set` not sorted on emit | `rows-test/unordered-containers-are-order-free` | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| R29 gate cycle check removed | `rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes`: the install row's child slot is forged to the root id, giving `:cyclic`. The mutant recurses without bound. This also refutes \"redundant with walk\": gate runs first, so walk never gets the chance. | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R30 carried code unchecked | `rows-test/task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame` (code map `{A [[:halt 99]]}` with a correct A → `:code`) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R33 non-portable literal accepted | `rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality` (`[:lit <byte payload>]` → `:literal`) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R35 decoded-equality duplicates unchecked | `rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality`: a set of two distinct `:vec` rows `[x [1]]` and `[x (1)]` → `:duplicate-key` | KILLED, 1 failure (on the second attempt, see below) |\n| R45 row-hash equality unchecked | `rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes` (root row body edited without rehash → status `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| R46 `:reference-kind` unchecked | `rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes` (bindings slot forged to the `:store` row → `:reference-kind`, which beats the hash mismatch the forgery also causes) | KILLED, 1 failure |\n\nRevert-mutants for the round-2 fixes:\n\n| Mutant | Killed by | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| F2a census key spelling not validated | `rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality` | KILLED, 5 failures |\n| F2b census sorted by spelling, not by n | `rows-test/census-is-numeric-from-ten-cells` | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| F3 gate's installs-slot check removed | `rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes` | KILLED, 1 failure |\n| F6a malformed marker-shaped map encoded as `[:lit]` | `rows-test/marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable` | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| F6b marker-shaped `[:lit]` decoded | `rows-test/marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable` | KILLED, 2 failures |\n| F7 present nil store-of accepted | `frame-test/rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper` (\"S4 nil store-of\") | KILLED, 1 failure |\n\nR35's first attempt **SURVIVED** (logged in the second batch JSON). That probe was a set `[[:cell x] [:lit [1]] [:lit (1)]]`, and decode step 5's `distinct` over the wire slots already refuses it as `:duplicate-key`, because `[:lit [1]] = [:lit (1)]` under host equality, so `check-abstract!` was never reached. The decoded-equality check in `check-abstract!` is reachable only when the difference is hidden inside distinct rows, so I nested it inside two distinct `:vec` rows. The re-run killed it (`6a-r2-mutants-R35.json`).\n\nFinding 1 has no JVM mutant: the defect exists only on ClojureDart.\n\n## Per finding\n\n1. **Dart failure at rows_test.cljc:238 (test change).** The multi-key `assoc` on the slot vector is now `(-> slots (assoc 2 …) (assoc 11 …) (assoc 13 …) (assoc 16 …))`. I searched both test files: there is no other multi-key `assoc` on a vector or nil. The remaining multi-key assocs (frame_test `reference-machines`, `tail-bookkeeping…`, the new `resume` helper) are on maps. **Dart is unverified by me; the orchestrator runs it.**\n2. **Census key escaped as a host exception (SOURCE change, rows.cljc).**\n   - `structural?` now takes the row kind. A `:C` slot of a `:cells` row must be a keyword whose name matches `c-([1-9][0-9]*)`, else `:slot-kind`.\n   - The EDN reader and the `clojure.edn` require are gone. `census-order` orders by `[digit-count digits]`, which is numeric order for decimal digit strings with no leading zero. It parses no number, so it cannot overflow.\n   - A non-matching key (reachable only from a partial `emit-numbered`) sorts last instead of throwing.\n   - Tests: keys `:x`, `:yin.k/c-01`, `:yin.k/c-0`, `:yin.k/c-` (rehashed via `replace-row`) and `(emit-numbered task {})` all refuse `:slot-kind`.\n3. **Gate read an unvalidated slot (SOURCE change, rows.cljc).** The gate's `:task-shape` condition now also requires `(vector? (nth slots 12))` and `(every? keyword? (nth slots 12))`. Test: installs slot forged to `5` → `:task-shape`.\n4. **Permutation test was vacuous (test change).** New `unordered-containers-are-order-free`:\n   - It uses the fixture and `reversed`, which lists every `:set` (the stream set and the cell set) and `:map` (outer and cursor-keyed) in reverse.\n   - It asserts that the two tasks differ, that their emit roots are equal, and that their `canonical-under` roots under the identity correspondence `{:s1 :s1 :s2 :s2}` are equal.\n   - It kills R26.\n5. **Canonical output was unpinned (test change).** New `canonical-roots-are-pinned`.\n   - **Root ids recomputed:** I got both of the reviewer's probe roots myself, and they are the same:\n     - `(fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)` → `:segment/blake3-be82d50b14804bb7a69103631b9c45759b49b67dbb3cebe75a1259fed6b4a937`\n     - `(frame->rows ft/worked-frame)` → `:segment/blake3-c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d`\n   - **Non-automorphic fixture:** cells l, a, b with equal bodies and the edge set `{[l l] [a b] [b a]}`. Refinement leaves all three cells in one class: every cell has the same positions, because each pair row's CE is equal under the initial labels. No automorphism maps l to a or b.\n   - **Its assertions:**\n     - The search is non-trivial: `{:search 2}` gives `:resource-limit`.\n     - The root is pinned: `:segment/blake3-8899f95d330a174ee098e10f16ecf2d017d5aae67d4f50d8369222342e6d36e9`.\n     - A renaming that also reverses the listed order gives the same root.\n     - The chosen leaf gives l the number `:yin.k/c-3`, and the renamed loop cell gets the same number.\n   - The pinned value is the implementation's output, cross-checked only by its renaming invariance and by R17/R21 being killed. I did not derive it by hand.\n   - The draft's own triangle/square fixture could not be used: see \"New finding\" above.\n6. **Marker representation (SOURCE change, rows.cljc, per ruling).**\n   - Markers stay plain maps. The namespace docstring now states the rule.\n   - **Encode:** a map whose sole key is `:yin.k/row` must carry an abstract row (`{:kind keyword :slots vector}`). A map whose sole key is `:yin.k/cell` must carry a keyword. Anything else of that shape is refused as status `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:marker`.\n   - **Decode:** any `[:lit m]` with `m` of that shape is refused the same way.\n   - My reading of the ruling: a guest map that *is* a well-formed marker cannot be told apart from a marker, so the default codec reads it as one. Only the malformed or literal collisions are refusable. The orchestrator should confirm this reading.\n   - Tests cover both directions, plus a positive case: `{:yin.k/row 1 :other 2}` round-trips.\n7. **`store-of nil` (SOURCE change, frame.cljc).** S4's `env?` now requires that a present `:yin.k/store-of` is non-nil, else `:env-shape`. Tested by \"S4 nil store-of\".\n8. **Census ordering (docstring + test).** The docstring records the reading: numeric by n, per draft §3, so `c-10` follows `c-9`. New `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cells` uses 10 cells, asserts the census order `c-1 … c-10`, and checks the decode/re-emit root.\n9. **Known limitation (note only):** `check-abstract!` (step-5 literal rules) runs only for `:task` roots. `rows->frame` therefore accepts frame-rooted packs that contain pure-literal collection rows or non-portable `[:lit …]`. 6b must never use `rows->frame` as a trust boundary.\n10. **Child budget: left unchanged.** It is not a one-token change: `emit-node` has no `budget` in scope (signature `[n numbering rows]`), so the budget would have to be threaded through `emit-node`, `emit-numbered`, `emit` and `frame->rows`.\n11. **Known limitation (note only):** frame defects inside a task are reported as `[root-id :frames i …keyed-path]` / `[root-id :parked p …]`, not draft §8's `[row-id slot-index …]`. Two path dialects coexist in one refusal family.\n12. **Test gaps:** all closed by the mutant table above.\n13. **Overclaims.**\n   - (b) There is no comment in the code making this claim; it was in the round-1 report. Corrected statement: acceptance item 2's task context is the fixture's setup `env` plus a constant `:ffi-call-ids #{}` (accurate for these stage-1 rows, which have no FFI). It is not decoded from the machine's tables.\n   - (c) Renamed `refinement-search-preserves-unordered-multiplicity` → `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cells-is-bounded`. It never exercised duplicate multiplicity.\n   - (d) `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` now does test only renaming, and its name is accurate. Container permutation has its own test (finding 4).\n   - (e) Findings 6 and 8 are now recorded in the docstring.\n\n## Additional observations (not fixed)\n\n- **Out-of-memory search (MEDIUM):** see \"New finding\" in the Outcome section.\n- **Possible false `:duplicate-key` (LOW, from reading the code, not run):** `check-abstract!`'s `value` maps `[:cell C]` to `[:identity C]`. A set that holds both `[:cell :yin.k/c-1]` and the literal vector `[:lit [:identity :yin.k/c-1]]` would therefore be refused as `:duplicate-key`, even though the two elements are distinct.\n\n## Lanes (all foreground, literal `-n`)\n\n| Lane | Command | Result | Log |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| JVM, 14 namespaces (same set as round 1) | `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.code-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.linearize-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.vm-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.effects-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.parity-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.link-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.load-paths-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.walker-trace-test -n yin.vm.ucf-test -n yin.vm.ucf.handoff-test -n yin.vm.ucf.safepoint-harness-test` | **191 tests, 4742 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0, all 14 \"Testing\" lines | `collab/6a-r2-jvm.log` |\n| JVM parity dependency | `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.parity-test` | 5 tests, 60 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors | `collab/6a-r2-jvm-parity.log` |\n| Node | `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge '{:ns-regexp \"^(yin\\\\.vm\\\\.semantic-register\\\\..*-test\\|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.ucf\\\\.(rows\\|handoff\\|safepoint-harness)-test\\|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.ucf-test)$\"}'` (pipes are unescaped in the actual command) | **196 tests, 4801 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, build 0 warnings; \"Testing\" lines include `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test` and `yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | `collab/6a-r2-node.log` |\n| G0 on final bytes | `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 26 tests, 522 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors | `collab/6a-r2-g0-final.log` |\n| kondo | `clj -M:kondo --lint <the four files>` | errors: 0, warnings: 0 (run twice, before and after the reflow) | console |\n| cljstyle | `mise exec -- cljstyle check <the four files>` | first run: exit 2, one let-binding in rows_test needed a reflow, which I applied; re-run: clean, exit 0 | console |\n| Dart | not run by me (orchestrator lane) | — | — |\n\n**Order of runs, plainly:**\n- The JVM 14-namespace and Node lanes ran *before* the whitespace-only cljstyle reflow of one `let` binding in `rows_test.cljc`.\n- After the reflow I re-ran G0 for the two changed namespaces, plus kondo, on the final bytes.\n- The other 12 namespaces and all source files did not change after the lane runs.\n\n**Refused commands:**\n- `cljstyle check <the four files>` (bare): \"This command requires approval\". I re-ran it as `mise exec -- cljstyle check …`, which worked.\n- Several compound shell lines were refused as \"multiple operations\" or \"variable can't be checked\". I split them into single commands; nothing was skipped.\n- A heredoc test append was refused (\"Contains brace with quote character\"). I appended with the edit tool instead.\n\n## Changed files (uncommitted)\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`: docstring (findings 6 and 8); `census-key` and `census-order`, and the `clojure.edn` require removed (finding 2); `structural?` takes kind (finding 2); gate installs check (finding 3); `marker-shaped?`, `value-node`, `decoded-value` (finding 6).\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`: `env?` refuses a present nil store-of (finding 7).\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame_test.cljc`: new `rule-boundaries-refuse-their-tamper`. The prescribed 24-row table is left intact.\n- `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`:\n  - finding 1 fix;\n  - assertions added to `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`, `schema-and-literal-canonicality` and `task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`;\n  - rename for finding 13(c);\n  - new tests `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `canonical-roots-are-pinned`, `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cells` and `marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable`.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 09:05:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 16:05:00 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Adversarial review of slice 6a (register frame validator and row codec)\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (non-author family). Author: codex gpt-6.1-sol.\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch `srvm-phase6a`, HEAD 4b0e735e, diff `master...HEAD` = exactly the four new files (frame.cljc 162 lines, rows.cljc 677, frame_test.cljc 281, rows_test.cljc 247). Nothing staged or committed by me; `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` exits 0 at the end of the review and both sources `cmp` equal to `collab/6a-reviewer-backups/{frame,rows}.cljc`.\n\n## What I ran (all foreground, all in this worktree)\n\n| Lane / check | Command | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| JVM baseline | `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 21 tests, 481 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| **Dart** (nobody had run it) | `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test,yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | compile OK; **21 tests: 20 passed, 1 FAILED** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`); log `collab/6a-reviewer-dart.log` |\n| Node | not re-run by me; the author's `collab/6a-node-final.log` shows `Testing yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test` and `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`, 191 tests / 4760 assertions / 0 failures |\n| Mutation campaign | `python3 -I collab/6a-reviewer-mutants.py` (44 mutants + 1 rerun), each `clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>` with literal names | 29 KILLED, 15 SURVIVED; per-mutant logs `collab/6a-reviewer-R*.log`, table `collab/6a-reviewer-mutants-main.json` (+ `6a-reviewer-mutants.json` for the R23 rerun) |\n| Derivation probe | temporary print-probe appended to rows.cljc (`collab/6a-reviewer-probe.cljc`), run through `rows-test/row-ids-are-jing-default`, restored | output `collab/6a-reviewer-P00.log` (80 `RVPROBE` lines) |\n\nBackups and restoration: my backups live in `collab/6a-reviewer-backups/`; the harness refuses to mutate unless each backup `cmp`-equals its source and the source is clean against HEAD, and after every mutant it restores from the backup and records `cmp` and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- <path>` (both 0 for all 45 runs). **Process incident, reported plainly:** my very first harness run applied the probe to rows.cljc before any reviewer backup existed (my earlier backup command had been refused by the sandbox as a whole, which I missed), and the harness crashed before restoring. I restored rows.cljc from the author's verified backup `collab/6a-verified-backups/rows.cljc` (`cmp` 0, `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` 0) before doing anything else; the guard above was added afterwards. The probe's output from that run is the one used below.\n\nThe sandbox refused every non-`clojure -M:test` way of evaluating project code (`clojure -Sdeps`, `clojure -M:cljs -i`, `java -cp`), so the \"independent derivations\" were done by hand on paper and compared against values printed by the probe; the probe computed Merkle ids for row sets I constructed by hand from the design tables, using only `jing/segment-key`.\n\n## Findings (severity | file:line | evidence | correction)\n\n1. **HIGH | test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:238 | Dart lane fails.** `task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame` throws `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable :yin.k/kind :extra-cell :yin.k/path [9]}` on Dart only. Cause: `(assoc (:slots base) 2 :blocked 11 ft/worked-code 13 [wait] 16 {...})` is a multi-key `assoc` on a **vector**; ClojureDart inlines it to `(-conj v {2 … 11 … 13 … 16 …})`, which appends the map as an 18th slot and leaves slot 13 (frames) empty, so the census cell `:test/cursor` is never reached (generated `test/cljd-out/yin/vm/ucf/rows-test_test.dart:4820-4835` shows the `PersistentHashMap` being `-conj`ed). The same trap is recorded in memory for nil; it applies to vectors too. Correction: chain single-key assocs (`(-> slots (assoc 2 :blocked) (assoc 11 ft/worked-code) (assoc 13 [wait]) (assoc 16 {...}))`). Production code is free of this (frame_test.cljc:120 and :273 multi-key assocs are on maps; rows.cljc has none). Acceptance item 11 therefore FAILS on Dart with a concrete failure, not just \"NOT RUN\".\n\n2. **MEDIUM | src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:119 | malformed census key escapes as a host exception.** `ordered-slots :cells` sorts by `(edn/read-string (subs (name k) 2))`; this runs at decode step 5 (`:row-order`, line 473) on wire data before numbering. Probe: a `:cells` row keyed `:x` (rehashed consistently) gives `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`, not `:yin.k/undecodable`. `emit-numbered` with a partial numbering fails the same way. Correction: validate `:C` slots of the census against `#\"c-[1-9][0-9]*\"` in `structural?` (refuse `:slot-kind`), and parse digits with a guard instead of the EDN reader.\n\n3. **MEDIUM | src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:424 | gate reads an unvalidated slot.** `gate` does `(doseq [install (nth slots 12)] …)` before the schema step checked slot kinds. Probe: installs slot `5` → `IllegalArgumentException: Don't know how to create ISeq from: java.lang.Long`. (The frames slot set to `5` is correctly refused `:slot-kind`.) Correction: in `gate`, require `(and (vector? installs) (every? keyword? installs))`, refusing `:task-shape`.\n\n4. **MEDIUM | test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:143-156 | acceptance item 10's \"unordered-container permutation\" is vacuous.** Mutant R26 (emit `:set` slots unsorted) SURVIVES the whole suite. Fixture `b` swaps the stream *identities* and the correspondence map \"accordingly\", so after substitution the set's elements are in the **same abstract order** as in `a`; the cursor-keyed map is resolved by refinement (`'one → x`), so its order is identical too. `refinement-search-preserves-unordered-multiplicity` cannot see it either: when every element is a cell, the exhaustive search picks the bytewise-min leaf and so compensates for an unsorted set. Correction: emit one task whose `:set`/`:map` slots are listed in reversed abstract order under the **identity** correspondence and assert equal roots (streams, not only cells, in the set).\n\n5. **MEDIUM | test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc | the canonical procedure's specific output is unpinned.** Mutants R21 (number classes n…1 instead of 1…n) and R17 (search keeps the first explored leaf instead of the bytewise-min) SURVIVE. No test pins a root id, and no fixture leaves a non-automorphic class after refinement (the draft §6 triangle/square case), so an implementation that is deterministic but disagrees with the design's procedure (or depends on hash-map traversal order in `search`) passes. Correction: pin at least one root id (probe values: fixture `(fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)` → `:segment/blake3-be82d50b14804bb7a69103631b9c45759b49b67dbb3cebe75a1259fed6b4a937`; `(frame->rows ft/worked-frame)` → `:segment/blake3-c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d`) and add a fixture with two refinement-equivalent but non-automorphic cells whose chosen leaf is asserted.\n\n6. **MEDIUM (6b boundary, decide now) | src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:534-536, 541-544 | in-band value markers.** The default `value-node`/`decoded-value` use plain maps `{:yin.k/row n}`/`{:yin.k/cell id}` as markers, so a *guest* map with exactly that key is misread. Probe: window value `{:yin.k/row 1}` round-trips to a `:row-shape` refusal; a map-shaped payload would be silently reinterpreted. §2.7's bijection does not hold on such frames. 6b's lift supplies its own classifier, but the default codec path (and `rows->frame`) should either use a distinct marker type (record/`deftype`) or refuse those maps as `:yin.k/non-portable`.\n\n7. **LOW | src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc:32 + rows.cljc:552-554, 604-608 | `store-of nil` breaks the identity.** A keyed frame with `:yin.k/store-of nil` present passes S4 (`closed?` allows the optional key with any value) but encodes to `[:env b nil]` and decodes without the key: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame ≠ id` (probe: `false`). Correction: S4 refuses a present nil `store-of` (`:env-shape`), or the design states canonical frames omit it.\n\n8. **LOW | rows.cljc:119 vs draft §3/§5 | census ordering ambiguity not recorded by the author.** Draft §3 says the census is \"sorted by n\"; draft §5's \"one ordering rule\" says sorted by canonical bytes of the key as spelled. They differ from ten cells on (`c-10` sorts before `c-2` bytewise). The implementation follows §3 (probe: ten cells emit `c-1 … c-10` numerically and decode). Record the reading; the suite has no ≥10-cell case.\n\n9. **LOW | rows.cljc:393 | step-5 literal rules skipped for frame-rooted packs.** `check-abstract!` runs only for `:task` roots, so `rows->frame` accepts a `:vec` row of pure literals or a non-portable `[:lit obj]`. Acceptable for a test device; note it so 6b never routes a frame-rooted pack to `rows->frame` as a trust boundary.\n\n10. **LOW | rows.cljc:151 vs 210 | child budget.** `emit-node` numbers an install child with `(canonical-numbering x)` (default budget) while `ce` passes the caller's `budget`; a tightened `:search`/`:work` bound does not reach children on emit.\n\n11. **LOW | rows.cljc:656-658, 674-676 | defect path vocabulary.** Frame defects are reported as `[root-id :frames i …keyed-path]`, not the draft §8's `[row-id slot-index …]`. Harmless, but two path dialects now exist in one refusal family.\n\n12. **LOW | test gaps shown by surviving mutants** (none is a code defect; the probe confirmed the code refuses correctly where it could be checked): R06 non-vector `:yin.k/k` (code gives `:k-shape`), R07 `pc 0` (code gives `:pc-bounds`), R10 A5-before-A6 order (code gives `:window-extra` for `{3 :x}`), R12 P2's empty admit set for `:park`/`:current-continuation`, R24 extra top-level wire key (code refuses `:row-shape`), R25 X1-before-frames order, R29 gate cycle check (redundant with `walk`), R30 malformed carried code (`:code`), R33 non-portable `[:lit …]` (`:literal`), R35 decoded-equality duplicate keys, R45 `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` (a stale id is still caught later as `:noncanonical-cells`, but the step-4 kind is never asserted), R46 `:reference-kind`. Each needs one assertion.\n\n13. **INFO | honesty of claims.** (a) The author's report is honest about Dart (NOT RUN) and about the resource-refusal spelling and the `debruijn/resolve-name` deviation; my hand derivation of row 21's chain agrees with `lexical-names` (`#{x f cat}`), so the deviation is sound. (b) Acceptance item 2 says the task context is \"decoded from the machine's own tables\"; the test takes `:free-env` from the fixture's setup `env` and a constant `:ffi-call-ids #{}` (frame_test.cljc:20, 124). Accurate for stage-1 rows (no FFI), but not what the sentence says. (c) `refinement-search-preserves-unordered-multiplicity` does not exercise duplicate multiplicity; the name overclaims. (d) `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` tests identity renaming, not container permutation (finding 4). (e) The author did not record the census-ordering ambiguity (finding 8) or the marker ambiguity (finding 6).\n\n## Answers to the six questions\n\n**1. Can each acceptance item's test fail?** Yes for items 1-9 (named killing mutants in the table): item 3's `each-rule-refuses-its-tamper` killed 12 rule mutants (R08, R11, R13, R14, R37-R44) and `first-defect-is-structural` killed the S2/S3 reorder (R02); item 4 killed R01 and R31; item 5 killed R09; item 6 killed R03 and R04; item 7 killed R19 and R31; item 8 killed R15 and R23; item 9 killed R27. Item 10: only R36 (missing correspondence) is killed; R17, R21 and R26 survive, so its permutation and canonicality clauses are not actually tested (findings 4, 5). Item 11 FAILS on Dart (finding 1). The author's nine mutants all map onto killed mutants of mine (M1≈R01/R31, M2≈R03, M4≈R13, M6≈R08, M7≈R28, M8≈R36, M9≈R32); M3 and M5 I did not replicate.\n\n**2. Rule coverage.** The tamper table has 24 frame rows plus X1 through `task-defect` = 25, each refused with its own kind (T1 and A1 legitimately share `:site`, A2/T1 share `:deliver`, as the design names them). The published input contract is plain data `{:free-env :parked-key :ffi-call-ids}` (frame.cljc:61-65). The `ns` form requires `yin.vm`, `.code`, `.analysis` only; no kernel, no `yin.vm.engine` (`yin.vm` is permitted by §3.1 and transitively loads dao.stream/dao.space.query, not the engine). Design order S→C→A/T→K→E→P is implemented; the only ordering mutant tried (R02) was killed.\n\n**3. Row codec.** Bijection holds on all 49 fixtures' frames (bijection test + R19/R31 killed) with classified values and a substituted pending; the evaluator's real pending fields are 6b's. Hand Merkle re-derivation of row 22's frame equals the codec root (below). Numbering: I reviewed `ce`, `positions`, `refine`, `search` against draft §6 steps 1-5 and found no encounter-order dependence (class order by label bytes, positions sorted, leaf chosen by min root body bytes; `ids` order only affects which automorphic cell gets which number, unobservable in the rows); renaming invariance confirmed by the probe (`{:x c-1 :y c-2}` vs `{:y c-1 :x c-2}` with equal roots). BLAKE3 only: no `sha` in either source; R23 (sha256) killed by the algorithm assertion. §5.5 re-emission is correspondence-based (R36 killed) but the permutation test is vacuous (finding 4). The exhaustive search's min-selection is untested (finding 5).\n\n**4. Independent derivations.** (a) Worked example `[:var 1 f] [:var 3 g] [:var 4 x] [:call 2 3 [4] false] [:var 5 y] [:call 0 1 [2 5] false] [:halt 0]`: L(5) = {1 2 5}, L(4) = use(4) ∪ (L(5) − def(4)) = {1 2}, saved(4,2) = {1}; matches the test and `analysis_test`. (b) Row 22 bare: act at pc 8 after `[:stream-next 3 4]`, rd 3, body registers 5, window keys {1 (the `cat` closure), 2 (the nil operand p)}, saved = {1 2}; design form `[:window 1 V 2 [:lit nil]]`. With opaque values I hand-built every row from the §2.3 tables (`:bindings` sorted by key bytes, `:env [bindings nil]`, `:window`, `:kframe [A pc env window rd]`, `:k`, `:act [A pc env window k rd]`, `:pending [:next :test/cursor]`, `:wait [act pending]`), hashed each with `jing/segment-key`, and got root `:segment/blake3-8c14cb536dd98a1c62cd00c3b577f11c23ed936d0d599186857d43fa3fb329d2` = the codec's root; the hand window row `[:window 1 [:lit :v] 2 [:lit nil]]` has id `…169e5b7a…` present in the wire, and the kframe's empty window is `[:window]` as §2.3 requires. (c) Row 21: body-ranges owners 13→`[x]`, 4→`[f]`, 0→`[cat]`, so C = {x f cat}; E = {cat f x 1 y 7}, free-env {x 99 y 7}; off C both are {y 7}; matches `lexical-names`. The tests pin no hash values (finding 5), so these were compared structurally and via the probe's ids.\n\n**5. Scope and portability.** Only the four new files changed; nothing of 4b/5 touched. No `#'` in sources or tests. Reader conditionals are `:cljd`-first. No `for`. Nil-valued keys preserved (R19/R31 killed). The one portability defect is the multi-key vector `assoc` (finding 1). `clojure.edn` is available on cljd (used elsewhere), but its use on wire data is finding 2.\n\n**6. Honest claims.** See finding 13. Nothing in the author's report claims a run that did not occur; my Dart run shows the sandbox block was environmental.\n\n## Mutant table\n\n| ID | File | Mutation | Named test | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R01 | frame | A6 `window-missing` dropped | frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member | KILLED (1/3 assertions, :208) |\n| R02 | frame | S2 and S3 reordered (window before K) | frame-test/first-defect-is-structural | KILLED (:224) |\n| R03 | frame | E2 lexical presence not required | frame-test/free-env-mismatch | KILLED (:218) |\n| R04 | frame | X1 reserved names unchecked | frame-test/free-env-mismatch | KILLED (:220) |\n| R05 | frame | P4 bookkeeping key membership dropped | frame-test/tail-bookkeeping-and-delivery | KILLED (:274) |\n| R06 | frame | S2 non-vector K accepted | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | SURVIVED |\n| R07 | frame | C2 `pc > 0` bound dropped | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | SURVIVED |\n| R08 | frame | C3 pc-body unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R09 | frame | A4 `window-self` dropped | frame-test/worked-example-saved | KILLED (:197) |\n| R10 | frame | A5/A6 order swapped | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | SURVIVED |\n| R11 | frame | E1 reserved binding unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R12 | frame | P2: `:park`/`:current-continuation` admit `:next` | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | SURVIVED |\n| R13 | frame | A2 destination equality dropped | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R14 | frame | K1 return-site unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R15 | rows | row id hashed without its first child slot | rows-test/row-ids-are-jing-default | KILLED (10 failures) |\n| R16 | rows | census extra cell not refused | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | KILLED (:202) |\n| R17 | rows | search keeps first explored leaf (input-order tie break) | rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant | SURVIVED |\n| R19 | rows | window nil-valued key dropped on encode | rows-test/bijection | KILLED (4 failures) |\n| R21 | rows | numbering reversed (cells swapped) | rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant | SURVIVED |\n| R22 | rows | `:rows` budget charge removed | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | KILLED (:208) |\n| R23 | rows | digest changed to sha256 (`{:algorithm :sha256}`; first attempt with a bare keyword was a no-op and is discounted) | rows-test/row-ids-are-jing-default | KILLED (26 failures) |\n| R24 | rows | extra top-level wire key accepted | rows-test/gates-and-graph-before-hashes | SURVIVED |\n| R25 | rows | X1 judged after waits | rows-test/numbering-precedes-frame-defect | SURVIVED |\n| R26 | rows | `:set` slots not sorted on emit | rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant | SURVIVED |\n| R27 | rows | row-order check removed | rows-test/window-sorted-and-unique | KILLED (:140) |\n| R28 | rows | `:noncanonical-cells` comparison removed | rows-test/numbering-precedes-frame-defect | KILLED (:195) |\n| R29 | rows | gate cycle check removed | rows-test/child-scope-and-shared-child-dag | SURVIVED |\n| R30 | rows | carried code well-formedness unchecked | rows-test/task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame | SURVIVED |\n| R31 | rows | `[:lit nil]` decoded as absence | rows-test/bijection | KILLED (8 failures) |\n| R32 | rows | search bound removed | rows-test/refinement-search-preserves-unordered-multiplicity | KILLED (2 failures) |\n| R33 | rows | non-portable literal accepted | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | SURVIVED |\n| R34 | rows | reference-free collection row accepted | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | KILLED (:203) |\n| R35 | rows | decoded-equality duplicate key unchecked | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | SURVIVED |\n| R36 | rows | missing stream correspondence guessed | rows-test/canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant | KILLED (:155) |\n| R37 | frame | P3 captured-site unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED (2) |\n| R38 | frame | P1 pending presence unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R39 | frame | T1 tail site unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R40 | frame | S1 closed key set unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED (2) |\n| R41 | frame | K3 return window unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED (2) |\n| R42 | frame | K2 return destination unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R43 | frame | C1 segment membership unchecked | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R44 | frame | S3 window keys not required to be registers | frame-test/each-rule-refuses-its-tamper | KILLED |\n| R45 | rows | row hash equality unchecked on decode | rows-test/window-sorted-and-unique | SURVIVED |\n| R46 | rows | structural reference kind unchecked | rows-test/schema-and-literal-canonicality | SURVIVED |\n\n44 mutants: 29 killed, 15 survived. Every run: one named test, foreground, restored from the reviewer backup, `cmp` 0 and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- <path>` 0.\n\n## Required before merge\n\n1. Fix rows_test.cljc:238 (single-key assocs) and re-run the Dart lane; the three-host gate is otherwise within reach (frame-test is fully green on Dart; the other 11 rows tests pass on Dart).\n2. Turn the two host exceptions in `decode` into refusals (findings 2, 3).\n3. Make item 10's permutation test actually permute (finding 4) and pin at least one root id plus a non-automorphic search fixture (finding 5).\n4. Decide the marker representation (finding 6); record the census-ordering reading (finding 8).\nThe remaining low items and the surviving-mutant assertions (finding 12) can land with the same follow-up.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
